::ALG2_U02_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 8 = 0. {
=x = 2 and x = 4 #Correct. Factoring gives (x - 2)(x - 4) = 0.
~x = -2 and x = -4 #These reverse the signs of the factors.
~x = 6 and x = 8 #These are coefficient/constant values, not zeros.
~x = 0 and x = 8 #This uses the constant but misses the factors.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + x - 12 = 0. {
~x = 4 and x = -3 #These signs are reversed.
=x = -4 and x = 3 #Correct. Factoring gives (x + 4)(x - 3) = 0.
~x = 1 and x = -12 #These use coefficient and constant values.
~x = 0 and x = -12 #These do not make the quadratic equal 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves x<sup>2</sup> - 16 > 0? {
~-4 < x < 4 #Between the zeros, the expression is negative.
~-4 <= x <= 4 #This includes the wrong interval.
=x < -4 or x > 4 #Correct. The upward-opening graph is above the x-axis outside the zeros.
~all real numbers #The expression is negative between -4 and 4.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves x<sup>2</sup> - 25 <= 0? {
~x < -5 or x > 5 #That is where the graph is above the x-axis.
~x <= -5 or x >= 5 #This includes outside values instead of the middle.
~all real numbers #Values far from 0 make the expression positive.
=-5 <= x <= 5 #Correct. The graph is on or below the x-axis between and including the zeros.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y < x<sup>2</sup> - 3, which statement is true? {
=Use a dashed boundary and shade below. #Correct. Strict inequality uses a dashed boundary, and y less than means below.
~Use a solid boundary and shade below. #Solid would include equality.
~Use a dashed boundary and shade above. #The boundary style is right, but shading is wrong.
~Use a solid boundary and shade above. #Both boundary style and shading are wrong.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y >= -x<sup>2</sup> + 1, which statement is true? {
~Use a dashed boundary and shade above. #The shading is right, but >= requires solid.
=Use a solid boundary and shade above. #Correct. The symbol includes equality, and y greater than means above.
~Use a solid boundary and shade below. #The boundary is right, but shading is wrong.
~Use no boundary. #A two-variable inequality needs a boundary.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 9 = 0. {
~x = 9 and x = -9 #These come before taking square roots.
~x = 0 and x = 9 #These do not satisfy the equation.
=x = -3 and x = 3 #Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~x = 3 only #Both positive and negative roots solve it.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 15 = 0. {
~x = 5 and x = -3 #These signs are reversed.
~x = -2 and x = 15 #These use coefficients, not zeros.
~x = 0 and x = -15 #These are not factor roots.
=x = -5 and x = 3 #Correct. Factoring gives (x + 5)(x - 3) = 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves x<sup>2</sup> - 4 >= 0? {
=x <= -2 or x >= 2 #Correct. The graph is on or above the x-axis outside the zeros and includes them.
~-2 < x < 2 #That interval gives negative values.
~-2 <= x <= 2 #That is where the expression is on or below zero.
~all real numbers #Values between -2 and 2 do not work.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves x<sup>2</sup> - 1 < 0? {
~x < -1 or x > 1 #Outside the zeros, the expression is positive.
=-1 < x < 1 #Correct. The graph is below the x-axis between the zeros, excluding endpoints.
~-1 <= x <= 1 #The endpoints make the expression 0.
~all real numbers #Outside values do not satisfy the inequality.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y <= x<sup>2</sup> + 4, how should the boundary be drawn? {
~dashed #Dashed is used for strict inequalities.
~vertical #The boundary is a parabola.
=solid #Correct. The symbol <= includes equality.
~not graphed #A boundary is needed.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y > -2x<sup>2</sup> + 5, where should the solution region be shaded? {
~below the parabola #Below would match y less than.
~only on the parabola #Strict inequality excludes the boundary.
~outside the zeros #That describes a one-variable interval.
=above the parabola #Correct. y greater than means points above the boundary.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> - 10x + 21 = 0. {
=x = 3 and x = 7 #Correct. Factoring gives (x - 3)(x - 7) = 0.
~x = -3 and x = -7 #These signs are reversed.
~x = 10 and x = 21 #These are coefficient/constant values.
~x = 0 and x = 21 #These do not make the equation true.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 8 = 0. {
~x = 2 and x = 4 #These signs are reversed.
=x = -2 and x = -4 #Correct. Factoring gives (x + 2)(x + 4) = 0.
~x = 6 and x = 8 #These are not roots.
~x = 0 and x = 8 #These do not satisfy the equation.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves -x<sup>2</sup> + 4 >= 0? {
~x <= -2 or x >= 2 #Outside the zeros, the expression is negative.
~-2 < x < 2 #This misses endpoints included by >=.
=-2 <= x <= 2 #Correct. The downward-opening graph is on or above the x-axis between the zeros.
~all real numbers #Values outside the interval do not work.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which solves -x<sup>2</sup> + 9 < 0? {
~-3 < x < 3 #Between the zeros, the expression is positive.
~-3 <= x <= 3 #This includes values that are not negative.
~all real numbers #Values between -3 and 3 do not work.
=x < -3 or x > 3 #Correct. The downward-opening graph is below the x-axis outside the zeros.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y = x<sup>2</sup> - 2 and y = 2, what equation finds intersection x-values? {
=x<sup>2</sup> - 2 = 2 #Correct. Set the two y-values equal to find where the graphs meet.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 2 = 0 #That finds x-intercepts of the parabola.
~x<sup>2</sup> = -2 #This does not set the functions equal correctly.
~2x = x<sup>2</sup> - 2 #This uses y = 2x, not y = 2.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y < -x<sup>2</sup> + 6, which boundary and shading are correct? {
~solid boundary, shade below #The shading is right, but strict inequality needs dashed.
=dashed boundary, shade below #Correct. Strict less-than excludes the boundary and shades below it.
~dashed boundary, shade above #The boundary style is right, but shading is wrong.
~solid boundary, shade above #Both boundary and shading are wrong.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x<sup>2</sup> + 5x = 0. {
~x = 5 only #This reverses the sign and misses x = 0.
~x = -5 only #This misses the factor x = 0.
=x = 0 and x = -5 #Correct. Factoring gives x(x + 5) = 0.
~x = 0 and x = 5 #The sign of the second solution is reversed.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 2x<sup>2</sup> - 8 = 0. {
~x = 4 and x = -4 #These come before square rooting.
~x = 2 only #Both positive and negative roots solve it.
~x = 0 and x = 8 #These do not satisfy the equation.
=x = -2 and x = 2 #Correct. Divide by 2 to get x<sup>2</sup> = 4.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval solves (x - 1)(x + 3) <= 0? {
=-3 <= x <= 1 #Correct. The product is nonpositive between the zeros and includes endpoints.
~x <= -3 or x >= 1 #That is where the product is nonnegative.
~-3 < x < 1 #This misses endpoints.
~all real numbers #The product is positive outside the zeros.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which interval solves (x - 4)(x + 2) > 0? {
~-2 < x < 4 #Between the zeros the product is negative.
=x < -2 or x > 4 #Correct. The product is positive outside the zeros.
~-2 <= x <= 4 #This includes the nonpositive interval.
~all real numbers #Values between -2 and 4 do not work.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y >= x<sup>2</sup> - 1, which point should be in the solution region? {
~(0, -2) #-2 is below -1.
~(2, 0) #At x = 2, the boundary is 3.
=(0, 0) #Correct. At x = 0, the boundary value is -1, and 0 >= -1 is true.
~(1, -2) #At x = 1, the boundary is 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>For y < x<sup>2</sup>, which point is a solution? {
~(2, 5) #5 is greater than 4.
~(0, 0) #0 < 0 is false because the inequality is strict.
~(-1, 2) #At x = -1, 2 < 1 is false.
=(2, 3) #Correct. At x = 2, x<sup>2</sup> = 4 and 3 < 4.
}

::ALG2_U02_L07_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.2; MLA.A2.PF.5; MLA.A2.PF.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation finds where x<sup>2</sup> - 4x and 0 intersect? {
=x<sup>2</sup> - 4x = 0 #Correct. Set the quadratic equal to the x-axis value 0.
~x<sup>2</sup> = 4 #This changes the expression.
~x - 4 = 0 only #This misses the factor x.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 4x = 0 #This changes the sign.
}

